speechtext:
I may say at once that
I agree with everything that has been said by the hon. member for St. Lawrence-St. George in support of occupational training for those who are unemployed in these years. As a matter of fact that was very much in the mind of the national employment commission when it made its recommendation with respect to youth training. The commission recommended the following as the basis upon which the plan should be framed:
Training projects of an occupational nature;
Short-term learnership courses, not exceeding one year in duration, devised to provide theoretical training concurrent with specific employment;
Work projects incorporating reconditioning, training and conservation features, for the purpose of restoring morale and conserving natural resources;
Training projects of a physical nature to assist in the maintenance of health and morale pending employment.
It will be seen that the commission, following in terms the recommendations of the youth employment committee, placed occupational training in the forefront of the program which it recommended to the government. When this plan was first put into operation, we made proposals to all provinces asking them to submit to us training projects that would fall within the various categories proposed by the national employment commission. At the same time we recognized, as the employment commission realized, that in view of the diversity of industry in the various provinces it would be desirable that the provinces themselves should decide where the emphasis should be placed with respect to one or another of these training plans.
Actually most of the provinces did in that first year, and later in the succeeding year, submit to us projects for occupational training. My hon. friend has referred particularly to the city of Montreal. The province of Quebec expressed its desire to cooperate in the youth training program and did submit a project for occupational training. Last year there was a schedule to the Quebec agreement providing for an expenditure of $225,000 for occupational training in that province. I am informed that up to the present time not more than $10,000 of that amount has been expended. I ought to say that we accepted without question the proposal of the Quebec government that this sum, $225,000, should be devoted to occupational

training, and that for obvious reasons. Quebec, like Ontario, is a great industrial province. Much of our manufacturing is centred there. We felt that in that province and in Ontario there was an exceptional opportunity to develop occupational training of our unemployed young people.
